+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Border Discontinuities:** Merging nodes of different levels can cause visual artifacts at cell boundaries. This is fixed by copying border data from adjacent cells [S1].
+*   **Texture Memory Limits:** The system must manage loading/unloading textures to fit within available texture memory [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Octree-Level Adaptive Rendering
-A technique that cuts rendering cost by rendering at coarser octree levels (moving up the octree) to match data resolution to image resolution, maintaining interactivity [S2]. It selectively reduces computation and data transfer based on visualization requirements [S2]. Performance: Adaptive rendering (e.g., level 6) provides enough high-level information and is generated about 3 times faster than high-resolution rendering (level 11) [S2]. It reduces both rendering time and data transfer from disk but does not change the effectiveness or scalability of 1DIP/2DIP strategies [S2].
+A technique that cuts rendering cost by rendering at coarser octree levels (moving up the octree) to match data resolution to image resolution, maintaining interactivity [S2]. It selectively reduces computation and data transfer based on visualization requirements [S2]. Performance studies show that adaptive rendering (e.g., level 6) can be generated about 3 times faster than high-resolution rendering (e.g., level 11) while providing sufficient high-level information [S2]. It reduces both rendering time and data transfer from disk but does not change the effectiveness or scalability of 1DIP/2DIP strategies [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the depth of the octree and the number of nodes in the active cut. Splitting/merging operations are $O(1)$ per node, but sorting the split queue is $O(N \log N)$ where $N$ is the number of non-leaf nodes [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the octree structure, the active cut, and the split/merge queues. Texture memory usage is proportional to the number of active leaf nodes [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Adaptive rendering can be significantly faster than high-resolution rendering (e.g., 3x faster) while maintaining visual fidelity [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Maximizes image quality within constraints; handles large datasets by loading only necessary data; maintains interactivity [S1, S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires complex data structures (queues, active cut); border discontinuities must be handled carefully; performance depends on system parameters (RAM, bandwidth) [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Interactive visualization of large unstructured grids where frame rate is critical [S1, S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When static, high-quality offline rendering is sufficient; when system resources are extremely limited and cannot support the overhead of the adaptation logic [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not explicitly given, but adaptive rendering is significantly faster than full high-resolution rendering (e.g., 3x speedup) [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the octree depth and texture memory constraints. The system loads only necessary textures [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Adaptive rendering provides enough high-level information for exploration while being much faster than full resolution [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Maximizes image quality within constraints; maintains interactivity; reduces data transfer [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires complex queue management and error metric calculation; potential for visual artifacts if border discontinuities are not handled [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When interactive exploration of large volumes is required, and system resources (CPU, GPU, memory) are limited [S1, S2].
+*   **When not to use:** When maximum visual fidelity is required regardless of performance, or when the volume is small enough to render at full resolution [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Originated from the need to visualize large unstructured grids interactively [S1]. The method was developed by Levenberg et al. (2002) as part of the "Interactive Visualization of Unstructured Grids Using Hierarchical 3D Textures" project [S1]. It builds on view-dependent continuous level-of-detail techniques for polygonal models [S1]. Later applied to parallel rendering of large time-varying volume data (e.g., earthquake simulations) by Yu et al. (2004) [S2].
+The method was introduced in the context of interactive visualization of unstructured grids using hierarchical 3D textures [S1]. It builds on concepts of view-dependent continuous level of detail for polygonal models [S1]. The approach was further applied to parallel rendering of large time-varying volume data, demonstrating its effectiveness in reducing I/O and rendering costs [S2].
 
